The transport route gives a rigorous spectral bound, but I did not obtain an inequality without a crossover scale.

Writing \(A=\sum_a c_av_a\), the CFT calculations give
\[
\frac{\operatorname{Var}(J)}L=\frac{\pi}{3}AT^3,
\qquad D_{\rm th}=\frac{\pi}{3}AT,
\]
with \(\operatorname{Re}\kappa=\pi D_{\rm th}\delta(\omega)+\cdots\), matching the [published convention](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10955-018-2025-x).

The main lattice result is
\[
0\le \frac{\langle[h_{-q},[H,h_q]]\rangle}{2L}\le 8M^3q^2.
\]
Controlled positive spectral matching at temperature \(T_*\), with lower factor \(\alpha\), therefore implies
\[
A\le \frac{48M^3}{\pi\alpha T_*^2}.
\]
This allows broadened transport peaks. Entropy, high-temperature convexity, and modular invariance do not remove the matching scale in the arguments examined.

